window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Language Families", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 Language Families A circuit drawing can say “apply H, then controlled-X, then measure.” A programming system must also decide what the qubits refer to, when measurements occur, and which instructions the hardware can execute. Language design makes these choices explicit. 1.1.1 Imperative and functional approaches An imperative description lists actions on named registers in time order. A functional description emphasizes composition of transformations and return values. Both can describe the same Bell preparation: create two zeros, apply H to the first, and controlled-X to the second. The programming style does not change the underlying state. Functional interfaces can make composition and resource use explicit, while imperative interfaces often resemble the circuit directly. 1.1.2 Standalone languages and embedded frameworks A standalone language has its own syntax and compiler; an embedded framework uses a host language to construct quantum instructions. Q# is a quantum programming language, OpenQASM describes circuit-level operations and classical control, and Qiskit is a Python SDK. A Python loop that appends gates runs while building a circuit; it is not automatically a loop executed by the quantum controller. Problem 1.1. Does assigning other = circuit create an independent circuit? Worked solution No. Both Python names refer to the same mutable object. Use circuit.copy() when subsequent edits must be independent. Neither action copies a physical quantum state."}, {"title": "Design Principles", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Design Principles 1.2.1 Types, ownership, and no-cloning A qubit handle names a physical or logical resource. Assigning another variable to that handle does not copy its quantum state. Linear or affine type disciplines can restrict duplicated use or disposal of resources; no-cloning is a physical constraint even when a language does not enforce it statically. A robust interface also prevents accidentally using the same wire as both independent inputs to a gate. 1.2.2 Reversibility, measurement, and classical control Unitary routines can have adjoints; a routine containing measurement or reset generally cannot be inverted as a unitary. Measurement produces a classical record that may select later operations. A host-language if statement on a circuit object cannot inspect an unexecuted qubit. Classical control during execution needs an instruction that refers to an actual recorded bit. Problem 1.2. Can every quantum function have an automatic inverse? Worked solution Only operations with a specified reversible action can be inverted this way. Measurement with a discarded outcome and reset lose information in the circuit-level description."}, {"title": "Abstraction and Execution", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 Abstraction and Execution 1.3.1 Circuit languages and intermediate representations An intermediate representation preserves the operations and dependencies while a compiler changes their implementation. Logical H and CNOT gates may become native rotations and entangling gates with inserted routing. The language should separate a circuit’s mathematical meaning from its device-specific realization. Exporting a circuit does not guarantee that every destination supports its timing or control constructs. 1.3.2 Qiskit as a Python SDK The companion builds a Bell circuit in Python, then asks a simulator for its state. The function returns a description, while Statevector performs a classical calculation of that description. The two roles are separate. Predict amplitudes (1/√2,0,0,1/√2) before running it; only then use the assertion as a check. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_01.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 1.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector\n\n# A Python function builds a circuit; it does not execute a quantum device.\ndef bell():\n    circuit = QuantumCircuit(2)\n    circuit.h(0)\n    circuit.cx(0, 1)\n    return circuit\n\ncircuit = bell()\nassert Statevector.from_instruction(circuit).equiv(\n    Statevector([2**-0.5, 0, 0, 2**-0.5]))\nprint(circuit.draw(output=\"text\")) Problem 1.3. Why distinguish construction time from execution time? Worked solution The host may expand a loop into a fixed gate sequence before any quantum run. A measurement-dependent loop requires controller support and cannot be resolved during ordinary circuit construction. We will use Python to build circuits and explicit execution interfaces to run them. The next chapter constructs a reproducible local environment. Sources and further study: OpenQASM language specification ; Selinger and Valiron, quantum lambda calculus . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "Python and Environments", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 Python and Environments A first program should have a result we can predict without the computer. Starting from zero, H makes equal amplitudes; measurement should give a fair coin. We will separate that prediction from random fluctuations in a finite run. 2.1.1 Python through circuit examples In circuit = QuantumCircuit(1) , assignment binds a Python name to a one-qubit circuit. A method call such as circuit.h(0) appends an instruction on qubit index zero. Parentheses supply arguments; indentation groups statements in functions and loops. A dictionary such as counts maps a measured bit string to the number of times it occurred. The program below uses these features only where the experiment needs them. 2.1.2 Packages, versions, and isolated environments Create an isolated environment rather than mixing course dependencies with unrelated projects. From the repository root, run the commands below. The requirements file pins the versions tested for this edition; the full environment lock records transitive packages. Use the accompanying TESTING.md for the tested Python version and platform notes. python3 -m venv .venv-quantum\nsource .venv-quantum/bin/activate\npython -m pip install -r education/programming/requirements.txt\npython education/programming/examples/chapter_02.py Problem 2.1. Why should a fair coin not be tested by demanding exactly 2048 ones? Worked solution Even an ideal fair source fluctuates. Exact equality would reject many correct executions. Test a distribution with a justified tolerance and test the unitary separately."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Ecosystem", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 The Qiskit Ecosystem 2.2.1 SDK, Aer, and Runtime The Qiskit SDK supplies circuit construction, quantum-information objects, transpilation, and reference primitives. Aer is a separate simulation package with noise models and additional simulation methods. IBM Runtime supplies remote execution services and associated primitives. Local examples need no cloud token; importing a remote client is not the same as submitting a job. 2.2.2 Notebooks and executable scripts A script executes from top to bottom in a fresh process. A notebook can retain variables from earlier cells, making a seemingly reproducible result depend on hidden execution order. Restart and run all cells when using notebooks, and keep the tested experiment in a script. Record versions, seeds, parameters, and shots; a seed fixes a particular pseudorandom experiment only within its implementation context. Problem 2.2. What does changing the seed change? Worked solution It changes the pseudorandom shot sequence, not the ideal quantum probability. A changed probability indicates a different circuit, model, or execution setting."}, {"title": "A First Program", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 A First Program 2.3.1 Build, run, and inspect The example creates H|0〉, adds measurement with measure_all(), and passes the resulting circuit to StatevectorSampler. The primitive returns a job; result()[0] selects its first workload result. The automatically created classical register is named meas, so data.meas.get_counts() returns its counts. The simulator internally computes amplitudes, but the requested result is a sampled classical record. 2.3.2 Predictions and reproducible results For R=4096 fair-coin shots, the expected count of one is 2048 and the standard deviation of its frequency is \\(1/(2\\sqrt R)=1/128\\). The assertion allows a much wider interval than that fluctuation. It checks a plausible distribution and total shot count, not an exact random sequence. A fixed seed helps regression testing, but a single passing sample is not a proof that H is correct. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_02.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 2.\"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom importlib.metadata import version\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\n\ncircuit = QuantumCircuit(1)\ncircuit.h(0)\ncircuit.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=7).run([circuit], shots=4096).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nassert sum(counts.values()) == 4096\nassert abs(counts.get(\"1\", 0) / 4096 - 0.5) < 0.05\nprint(\"Python\", sys.version.split()[0], \"Qiskit\", version(\"qiskit\"))\nprint(counts) Problem 2.3. Where are amplitudes measured in this example? Worked solution They are not physically measured. The reference simulator computes them classically to generate samples. Hardware can provide measurement outcomes, not its complete internal state vector. The next circuit has two qubits. Its correlations are simple, but its bit ordering must be made explicit. Sources and further study: IBM, SDK primitive simulation ."}, {"title": "Registers and Gates", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 Registers and Gates Prepare a Bell pair and measure it. Only equal bits should appear, but that symmetric example can conceal an ordering mistake. We will add an asymmetric test before trusting the program. 3.1.1 Quantum and classical registers Qubits carry quantum state; classical bits receive measurement records. QuantumCircuit(2) creates two quantum wires, indexed zero and one. Appending H on zero and CX(0,1) means the first is the control and the second the target. A two-qubit state here is written |q1 q0〉; the leftmost displayed ket bit is not the first Python index. 3.1.2 Gate order and circuit composition Instructions are appended in chronological order. In a matrix product, the last applied gate appears on the left. Circuit composition appends another circuit with an explicit wire mapping. A circuit box may stand for many elementary gates; decomposition reveals its contents, while transpilation additionally adapts them to a gate set and connectivity. Problem 3.1. Would counts containing only 00 and 11 prove entanglement? Worked solution No. A classical mixture of those states has the same computational-basis distribution. An XX measurement can distinguish that mixture from the ideal Bell state."}, {"title": "Measurement", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 Measurement 3.2.1 Mapping qubits to classical bits Measurement maps a selected quantum wire to a selected classical bit. measure_all() adds a register and measures every qubit, whereas measure(qubit, clbit) lets you choose the mapping. Count strings describe the classical storage, so changing that mapping changes how the result should be decoded even when the physical state is unchanged. 3.2.2 Bit strings and tensor-product ordering Qiskit’s integer convention makes qubit zero the least significant bit. Applying X only to qubit zero gives |01〉 and state-vector index one. The label ZI acts on qubit one, while IZ acts on qubit zero. Reversing a drawing for visual convenience does not automatically reverse these mathematical conventions. The asymmetric check in the companion catches confusion that Bell-state counts cannot expose. Problem 3.2. What state results from X on qubit one alone? Worked solution It is |10〉, with state-vector index two. This is the opposite single-bit position from X on qubit zero."}, {"title": "Circuit Inspection", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 Circuit Inspection 3.3.1 Drawing and decomposing circuits A text circuit drawing is available without plotting dependencies. Read its wires from left to right in time, but read a returned bit string according to its classical register ordering. Inspect gate counts and decompositions when a familiar-looking box hides arithmetic or multi-controlled gates. Visual compactness is not a resource bound. 3.3.2 Comparing code with predicted evolution The Bell state is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). Ideal measurements therefore never return 01 or 10. Counts fluctuate between 00 and 11. The companion checks support, then uses X on only qubit zero to verify index order. Testing a second measurement basis would additionally distinguish a coherent Bell pair from a classical mixture. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_03.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 3.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector\n\nbell = QuantumCircuit(2)\nbell.h(0)\nbell.cx(0, 1)\nbell.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=8).run([bell], shots=1024).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nassert set(counts) <= {\"00\", \"11\"}\n# An asymmetric check is essential: a Bell state hides reversed bit order.\nasymmetric = QuantumCircuit(2)\nasymmetric.x(0)\nassert Statevector.from_instruction(asymmetric).probabilities_dict() == {\"01\": 1.0}\nprint(bell.draw(output=\"text\"))\nprint(counts) Problem 3.3. Why include a text drawing in a runnable example? Worked solution It makes the appended gate order and wire targets inspectable. The numerical test still supplies the stronger check of the circuit’s action. With the indexing fixed, we can compare circuits directly with their mathematical states and operators. Sources and further study: IBM, bit ordering . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "State Representations", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 State Representations A simulator can show the state hidden inside a circuit. Used carefully, this is a debugging instrument: it lets us compare the program with the predicted mathematical action before introducing sampling or hardware noise. 4.1.1 State vectors and density matrices A state vector lists complex amplitudes in a fixed basis. A density matrix also describes uncertainty and subsystems: for a pure vector ψ it is ψψ†, while a mixture averages these outer products. Statevector.from_instruction evolves an initially zero register through a suitable circuit. DensityMatrix provides the more general representation. Neither is a promise that a hardware run can reveal all those numbers. 4.1.2 Composite systems and subsystem order For a Bell pair, tracing out qubit one leaves the other in I/2. partial_trace takes the subsystem indices to discard, not the indices to keep. Its result is not the plus state even though both have fair Z measurements. The plus state has off-diagonal entries 1/2; I/2 does not. The distinction appears in an X-basis test. Problem 4.1. What does partial_trace(state,[1]) keep for two qubits? Worked solution It discards subsystem one and keeps subsystem zero. Confusing the two can pass unnoticed on symmetric states."}, {"title": "Operators and Observables", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 Operators and Observables 4.2.1 Unitary operators Operator(circuit) constructs the circuit’s matrix when the circuit has a suitable unitary description. A unitary U satisfies U†U=I, and the companion checks this numerically. Matrices grow as 4^n complex entries, so this is a small-system verification technique. An exact matrix calculation is not a scalable simulation strategy for arbitrary large circuits. 4.2.2 Pauli observables and expectation values A Pauli observable has outcomes ±1. For the Bell state, XX and ZZ have expectation one, while ZI has expectation zero. SparsePauliOp stores sums of Pauli strings and coefficients without necessarily constructing a dense matrix. In a multi-qubit string, the rightmost character acts on qubit zero. The simulator computes an expectation directly; an experiment estimates it from repeated measurements. Problem 4.2. Does a zero expectation of Z imply a maximally mixed state? Worked solution No. Plus, minus, and many other states have zero Z expectation. Additional observables are needed to determine the state."}, {"title": "Simulation", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 Simulation 4.3.1 Exact evolution and sampled results An exact expectation and a sampled mean answer different questions. The former checks ideal mathematical action; the latter models finite measurement records. A density-matrix noise simulation adds another layer. Keep all three results separately so a discrepancy can be traced to code, sampling, or the noise model rather than attributed vaguely to “quantum randomness.” 4.3.2 Simulator access versus physical measurement The companion verifies Bell correlations, the reduced state, and unitarity. Its tolerances address floating-point arithmetic. Extend it by changing H to an Ry rotation and predicting the reduced probabilities cos²(θ/2), sin²(θ/2) before running. Off-diagonal terms remain absent locally after the entangling CNOT, even though the global state is pure. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_04.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 4.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector, DensityMatrix, Operator, SparsePauliOp, partial_trace\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.h(0)\nqc.cx(0, 1)\nstate = Statevector.from_instruction(qc)\nlocal = partial_trace(DensityMatrix(state), [1])\nassert np.allclose(local.data, np.eye(2) / 2)\nassert np.isclose(state.expectation_value(SparsePauliOp(\"XX\")), 1)\nassert np.isclose(state.expectation_value(SparsePauliOp(\"ZI\")), 0)\nassert np.allclose(Operator(qc).data.conj().T @ Operator(qc).data, np.eye(4))\nprint(\"Local state:\", local.data) Problem 4.3. Why avoid dense matrices in the large-instance benchmark? Worked solution Their classical construction may dominate the experiment exponentially. Use them as small-instance correctness checks, and report scalable resource counts separately. Next we give circuit fragments explicit interfaces so they can be safely reused and inverted. Sources and further study: IBM, quantum information API . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "Circuit Interfaces", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-1", "text": "5.1 Circuit Interfaces A useful circuit fragment should behave predictably when embedded in a larger computation. Its interface must specify wire order, parameters, and workspace, just as a classical function specifies its inputs and outputs. 5.1.1 Python circuit factories A circuit factory is a Python function returning a newly constructed circuit. The rotation factory below makes one Ry gate with a supplied angle. Fresh objects prevent one experiment’s edits from silently changing another. Returning a circuit also lets the caller decide where it is composed and when it is executed. 5.1.2 Register contracts and composition The interface of a predicate may be “input wires first, target next, zeroed scratch last; preserve inputs and clear scratch.” Document such contracts beside the factory. compose can map the component’s wires onto selected wires of a larger circuit. A valid mathematical component on the wrong wire mapping is still a program bug. Problem 5.1. Why return a fresh circuit instead of a shared global one? Worked solution Appending gates mutates circuit objects. A shared object can accumulate instructions across calls and make experiments depend on call history."}, {"title": "Reusable Operations", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-2", "text": "5.2 Reusable Operations 5.2.1 Custom gates and controlled gates to_gate() packages a unitary circuit into a reusable gate, and control() constructs a controlled version when supported. Control is a mathematical construction whose decomposition can be costly. It is not equivalent to using a Python if statement. A global phase of the base operation becomes a relative phase when controlled and must be preserved. 5.2.2 Inverses and parameter binding Parameter(\"theta\") creates a symbolic parameter. assign_parameters binds it to a numerical value without replacing the entire circuit construction. inverse() reverses a reversible circuit; a circuit containing measurement generally has no unitary inverse. The companion composes a bound rotation with its inverse and compares the result with the identity matrix. Problem 5.2. Does U followed by U† test the intended specification? Worked solution It checks reversibility and the implementation of the inverse, but a wrong U can also pass. Compare with independently predicted outputs or matrices. A one-parameter entangled trial state: cos(θ/2)|00⟩+sin(θ/2)|11⟩. Measure ZZ directly or rotate both wires with H before measuring XX."}, {"title": "Workspace Contracts", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-3", "text": "5.3 Workspace Contracts 5.3.1 Ancilla allocation Ancillas are extra quantum wires, not temporary Python variables. Allocate them explicitly and state their required initial state. A borrowed qubit in an unknown state is a different resource from a clean zero ancilla. Simply stopping references to a wire does not physically erase its correlation with the data. 5.3.2 Uncomputation and cleanup checks Check workspace restoration on coherent inputs, not just individual values. After a clean subroutine, the workspace must factor into its specified state, with the correct relative phases on the useful registers. A probability-zero error in scratch measurement is useful but does not alone certify the target action. The next oracle chapter gives a full coherent sign check. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_05.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 5.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nangle = Parameter(\"theta\")\ndef rotation(theta):\n    qc = QuantumCircuit(1, name=\"rotation\")\n    qc.ry(theta, 0)\n    return qc\n\ncomponent = rotation(angle)\nbound = component.assign_parameters({angle: 0.7})\nidentity = bound.compose(bound.inverse())\nassert np.allclose(Operator(identity).data, np.eye(2))\ncontrolled = bound.to_gate().control(1)\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.append(controlled, [0, 1])\nprint(qc.draw(output=\"text\")) Problem 5.3. Can a symbolic circuit be sampled before all necessary parameters are bound? Worked solution Only if parameter values are supplied through a supported execution interface. Otherwise its numerical evolution is unspecified and execution should reject it. Reusable parameterized circuits become numerical experiments through sampling and estimation primitives. Sources and further study: IBM, QuantumCircuit API ."}, {"title": "Execution Primitives", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-1", "text": "6.1 Execution Primitives For Ry(θ)|0〉, the probability of one is sin²(θ/2), while the mean of Z is cos θ. These are two ways to ask about the same circuit, and they correspond to two execution interfaces. 6.1.1 V2 Sampler and Estimator A Sampler accepts measured circuits and returns shot records. An Estimator accepts circuits together with observables and returns expectation estimates. V2 interfaces group related work in primitive unified blocs, usually abbreviated PUBs. A PUB is an input grouping, not a quantum gate or a physical batch guarantee. The companion uses local Statevector implementations, so no device account is involved. 6.1.2 Local implementations and result structures The Estimator input tuple contains a circuit, an observable, and parameter values. The result is indexed by input PUB; data.evs holds the corresponding expectation array. For Sampler, data is organized by classical register name; measure_all creates meas in our examples. Explicitly named registers change the field you read. Do not paste a result-access expression without checking the circuit’s register names. Problem 6.1. At θ=π/3, predict p(1) and the Z mean. Worked solution They are 1/4 and 1/2. A result near zero would suggest a wrong angle, observable, or parameter mapping."}, {"title": "Statistical Readout", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-2", "text": "6.2 Statistical Readout 6.2.1 Shots, precision, and uncertainty A Bernoulli frequency has variance \\(p(1-p)/R\\), while an average of ±1 values has variance \\((1-\\langle Z\\rangle^2)/R\\). The identity \\(\\langle Z\\rangle=1-2p(1)\\) connects them. Exact StatevectorEstimator at zero requested precision has no shot fluctuation for our unitary examples; it is not a model of a hardware estimator with finite shots. 6.2.2 Bit strings and classical postprocessing Decode counts using the measurement mapping before converting them into an observable. For two bits, ZZ assigns +1 to equal bits and −1 to unequal bits. Summing signed counts and dividing by total shots estimates its mean. A normalized count table is an empirical distribution; its small entries have large relative uncertainty. Problem 6.2. How does doubling shots change a standard error? Worked solution It reduces the standard error by √2, assuming independent identically distributed shots. It does not remove systematic bias."}, {"title": "Repeated Workloads", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-3", "text": "6.3 Repeated Workloads 6.3.1 Parameter sweeps The companion sends angles 0, π/2, and π as a three-row parameter array. Its returned Z means must be 1, 0, and −1. Parameter sweeps reuse a circuit structure while changing values. Array shapes describe the experiment and should be checked explicitly; silently broadcasting the wrong dimension can produce a different workload. 6.3.2 Batching inputs and interpreting results Batching several experiments can reduce host overhead, but each requested circuit, parameter set, observable group, and shot budget still consumes resources. Keep the parameter order and result order in the saved record. The example separates an exact three-point sweep from a sampled midpoint, so the two sources of output are unambiguous. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_06.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 6.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler, StatevectorEstimator\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import SparsePauliOp\n\ntheta = Parameter(\"theta\")\nqc = QuantumCircuit(1)\nqc.ry(theta, 0)\nvalues = [[0], [np.pi / 2], [np.pi]]\nresult = StatevectorEstimator().run([(qc, SparsePauliOp(\"Z\"), values)]).result()\nassert np.allclose(result[0].data.evs, [1, 0, -1])\nmeasured = qc.assign_parameters({theta: np.pi / 2})\nmeasured.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=9).run([measured], shots=4096).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nprint(\"Exact means:\", result[0].data.evs, \"sampled counts:\", counts) Problem 6.3. Why keep a circuit without measurements for the Estimator? Worked solution It represents the prepared state whose observable is requested. The estimation implementation chooses its measurement procedure; terminal measurements in the input can conflict with that contract. We can now run and interpret a circuit. The next step is to construct the input-dependent oracle that an algorithm assumes. Sources and further study: IBM, StatevectorEstimator ; IBM, StatevectorSampler ."}, {"title": "Reversible Predicates", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-1", "text": "7.1 Reversible Predicates The oracle f(a,b,c)=ab XOR c is small enough to verify exhaustively and rich enough to expose the distinction between a Boolean truth table and a coherent circuit. 7.1.1 Boolean evaluation Assign a,b,c to qubits zero, one, and two, and the answer target to qubit three. A Toffoli controlled by zero and one toggles the target by ab; CNOT from two toggles it by c. Inputs are retained and the target is XORed with f. No scratch is needed for this predicate. In a larger predicate, the compute–copy–uncompute construction supplies cleanup. 7.1.2 Answer registers and retained inputs The full contract applies for target zero and target one. Testing only a zero target can miss an implementation that initializes or overwrites the answer irreversibly. The companion checks all sixteen basis columns against the required permutation. Basis columns with their complex phases determine the whole linear operation; basis probabilities alone do not. Problem 7.1. What happens to an answer target initially one when f=1? Worked solution It becomes zero. The answer operation is XOR, not assignment."}, {"title": "Arithmetic Circuits", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-2", "text": "7.2 Arithmetic Circuits 7.2.1 Addition and comparison A two-bit incrementer applies CX from the low bit to the high bit, then X on the low bit, producing addition modulo four. A comparator can compute whether a−b borrows, retain the flag, and reverse the arithmetic. Explicitly choose modular or overflow-preserving behavior. The bit width is part of the function; it cannot be inferred from an unbounded mathematical integer. 7.2.2 Precision and workspace requirements Fixed-point arithmetic stores a scaled integer, so changing width changes both range and precision. Extra carries, comparison flags, and temporary products require wires and cleanup. Counting only the final phase or output gate hides most of the oracle implementation. For a finite table, state preparation or memory access also needs a specified circuit. Problem 7.2. Why test a target minus state? Worked solution It turns function values into relative phases and exposes phase or cleanup errors that may be invisible on measured basis inputs."}, {"title": "Phase Oracles", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-3", "text": "7.3 Phase Oracles 7.3.1 Compute, phase, and uncompute Preparing the answer target in minus converts the bit oracle to a sign oracle. Every input amplitude gains (−1)^f, while the target remains minus. The companion prepares all eight inputs uniformly and compares every final complex amplitude with this prediction. This catches unwanted phases and retained records that a histogram test could miss. 7.3.2 Superposition tests and resource counts The resource count for this implementation is one Toffoli and one CNOT, plus preparation and readout if used. A Toffoli is not native on every backend, so report both this logical count and its compiled decomposition. The circuit’s simple contract makes it usable as an independent component in search, phase encoding, or a larger arithmetic calculation. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_07.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 7.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\n# f(a,b,c) = ab XOR c; q0=a, q1=b, q2=c, q3=target.\nqc = QuantumCircuit(4)\nqc.ccx(0, 1, 3)\nqc.cx(2, 3)\nmatrix = Operator(qc).data\nfor index in range(16):\n    a, b, c = index & 1, (index >> 1) & 1, (index >> 2) & 1\n    expected = index ^ (((a & b) ^ c) << 3)\n    assert np.allclose(matrix[:, index], np.eye(16)[:, expected])\n# Target minus makes the same oracle a phase oracle.\nprep = QuantumCircuit(4)\nprep.h([0, 1, 2])\nprep.x(3)\nprep.h(3)\nstate = Statevector.from_instruction(prep.compose(qc))\nexpected = []\nfor index in range(16):\n    a, b, c, target = [(index >> k) & 1 for k in range(4)]\n    expected.append((-1)**((a & b) ^ c ^ target) / 4)\nassert np.allclose(state.data, expected)\nprint(\"All basis columns and coherent signs verified\") Problem 7.3. Is a four-bit adder an implementation of arbitrary-precision integer addition? Worked solution No. Its range and overflow behavior are fixed. A scalable implementation specifies a family parameterized by width and gives its resource growth. With verified oracles, the algorithms course can be translated into executable constructions. Sources and further study: IBM, circuit API . Phase-oracle readout. U_f is the reversible bit oracle on the input register and the minus target, not an elementary gate. The final input measurement reads the encoded pattern."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-1", "text": "8.1 Fourier Methods Implement two familiar transformations and compare their matrices or output probabilities with independent formulas. Small examples are useful for debugging, but the construction must keep its conventions explicit. 8.1.1 Constructing the Fourier circuit Our two-qubit Fourier transform uses the positive exponential exp(2πixy/4)/2. The code applies H to qubit one, a controlled π/2 phase, H to qubit zero, and a swap. The matrix assertion compares every entry with the mathematical transform. Omitting the swap changes output bit order; taking the inverse changes the exponent sign. 8.1.2 Controlled powers and phase estimation Phase estimation prepares a control register, applies controlled powers of a chosen unitary, then inverse QFT. A power implemented by repeating U costs that many U uses. When testing, choose an eigenstate with an exactly representable phase before testing approximate phases or superpositions. Decode the control bits using the register mapping, not the visual wire order alone. Problem 8.1. Why compare QFT matrices instead of only transforming zero? Worked solution Both forward and inverse Fourier transforms map zero to the same uniform state. A zero-input test misses the sign convention. Positive-sign two-qubit Fourier circuit. The final SWAP exchanges the two wires; its connected endpoints are a two-wire operation. The controlled phase applies only when both bits are one."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-2", "text": "8.2 Amplitude Manipulation 8.2.1 Preparation and reflections For four items with only 11 marked, begin uniformly and apply CZ to negate the marked amplitude. A prepared-state reflection follows. The reflection sequence used in the companion differs by a global sign from one common convention; the success probability is unaffected. This phase convention would matter if the whole iteration were later coherently controlled. 8.2.2 Search and amplification One Grover iteration gives unit success for one marked item out of four. The companion checks probability at index three. For larger instances, choose iterations from the initial success probability or use a justified unknown-success schedule. More iterations can reduce success, so a loop that runs “until enough gates” is not an algorithm. Problem 8.2. What happens after a second Grover iteration in the four-item example? Worked solution The success returns to one quarter. The rotation has passed the good direction."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-3", "text": "8.3 Quantum Walks 8.3.1 Position and coin registers A coined walk uses a coin register and a position register. Implement a coin rotation followed by reversible conditional increment and decrement modulo the graph size. Keep the coin: discarding its direction information after each step changes the evolution. A non-power-of-two position space also needs a policy for unused basis states so the full circuit remains unitary. 8.3.2 Implementation and classical baselines A meaningful benchmark compares the same access and output tasks. Verify small matrices, then measure compiled gates, depth, qubits, oracle calls, and readout costs as size grows. A classical simulator runtime is a property of the simulation method, not evidence that the quantum algorithm is slow or fast on quantum hardware. The two examples below establish correctness checks, not a performance advantage. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_08.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 8.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\n# Positive-sign QFT on integer labels |q1 q0>.\nqft = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqft.h(1)\nqft.cp(np.pi / 2, 0, 1)\nqft.h(0)\nqft.swap(0, 1)\nexpected = np.array([[np.exp(2j*np.pi*x*y/4)/2 for x in range(4)] for y in range(4)])\nassert np.allclose(Operator(qft).data, expected)\n# One Grover step for marked state |11> in four items.\nsearch = QuantumCircuit(2)\nsearch.h([0, 1])\nsearch.cz(0, 1)\nsearch.h([0, 1]); search.x([0, 1]); search.cz(0, 1)\nsearch.x([0, 1]); search.h([0, 1])\nassert np.isclose(Statevector.from_instruction(search).probabilities()[3], 1)\nprint(qft.draw(output=\"text\")) Problem 8.3. Can the position register alone generally implement a reversible coined shift? Worked solution The direction label is needed to distinguish the routes. Removing it without another reversible encoding can merge states and invalidate the proposed unitary. The examples suggest a testing discipline. The next chapter makes that discipline systematic. Sources and further study: IBM, quantum information operators ."}, {"title": "Functional Tests", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-1", "text": "9.1 Functional Tests A wrong circuit can pass every computational-basis probability test. Identity and Z are the simplest example: they preserve basis labels but disagree on plus. Verification must test the kind of behavior the component promises. 9.1.1 Basis inputs and superpositions A basis test should compare complex output amplitudes when checking a coherent contract. If it compares only measured labels, it may miss input-dependent phases. Superpositions make those phases visible. The companion first confirms that I and Z have identical basis output probabilities, then shows that their actions on plus are inequivalent. 9.1.2 Equivalence and global phase A whole circuit can differ by one global phase without changing its standalone output statistics. Operator.equiv recognizes that freedom. An independent phase on each input column is not a global phase. Moreover, controlling a unitary turns its global phase into a relative control phase. The companion checks both cases, so a tolerance or equivalence shortcut does not erase meaningful distinctions. Problem 9.1. Why does testing Z on zero miss the bug? Worked solution Zero is an eigenstate of Z with eigenvalue one. Test plus to expose the relative sign between zero and one."}, {"title": "State and Workspace Checks", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-2", "text": "9.2 State and Workspace Checks 9.2.1 Register ordering and ancilla cleanup Use asymmetric inputs to test ordering. Check the probability of nonzero scratch outcomes, the reduced target state, and the final joint state when small enough. A clean workspace should factor from the useful registers. Basis tests for a fixed initialized workspace certify only that subspace, which is appropriate if the interface explicitly requires that initialization. 9.2.2 Inverse and composition tests Test inverse and composition identities, but pair them with a specification test. U followed by its computed inverse is identity even if U implements the wrong function. Composition can also reveal incompatible wire contracts or omitted phase conventions. Random complex inputs are useful additional checks, not a substitute for a proof or a complete small matrix test. Problem 9.2. Can two individually global-equivalent gates always be substituted under control? Worked solution No. Their differing phase acts only in the control-one branch and becomes observable relative to the control-zero branch."}, {"title": "Statistical Tests", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-3", "text": "9.3 Statistical Tests 9.3.1 Numerical tolerances Numerical tolerances should reflect floating-point accumulation and approximation error. Exact small gates can use tight absolute tolerances such as 10^-12; an intentionally approximate product formula needs its analytic error budget. Relative tolerance alone is unsuitable near a zero amplitude. Report which equivalence notion and norm the test uses. 9.3.2 Sampling uncertainty and regression tests Sampling tests need statistical tolerances and can occasionally fail even for correct code. Fix seeds for regression reproducibility, but also check deterministic state or operator predictions. Avoid demanding exact counts from an ideal probabilistic experiment. The companion intentionally includes a wrong circuit and verifies that the meaningful coherent test rejects it. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_09.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 9.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\nidentity = QuantumCircuit(1)\nwrong = QuantumCircuit(1)\nwrong.z(0)\n# Basis output probabilities match, but relative phase differs.\nfor label in (\"0\", \"1\"):\n    state = Statevector.from_label(label)\n    assert np.allclose(state.evolve(identity).probabilities(), state.evolve(wrong).probabilities())\nassert not Statevector.from_label(\"+\").evolve(identity).equiv(Statevector.from_label(\"+\").evolve(wrong))\nassert not Operator(identity).equiv(Operator(wrong))\nphase = QuantumCircuit(1)\nphase.global_phase = 0.3\nassert Operator(identity).equiv(Operator(phase))\nassert not Operator(identity.to_gate().control()).equiv(Operator(phase.to_gate().control()))\nprint(\"Relative-phase bug detected; controlled global phase also checked\") Problem 9.3. Is a larger tolerance always safer? Worked solution It reduces false failures but can accept real bugs. Choose it from the numerical and approximation budgets, and include known incorrect examples that should fail. The next transformation is performed by a compiler. Its output needs the same semantic checks, with layout taken into account."}, {"title": "Backend Targets", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-1", "text": "10.1 Backend Targets A circuit requests CNOT between qubits zero and two, but the device has only a line of neighboring qubits. Compilation must change the implementation while preserving the logical computation. 10.1.1 Native operations A backend target describes supported operations and the qubits on which they can act, often with timing and calibration information. A list of native gate names is not enough to describe all device constraints. The example uses a simple line coupling graph and an explicit gate basis so the routing problem is visible without contacting hardware. 10.1.2 Connectivity and device constraints Connectivity can force swaps or equivalent routing operations. Native entangling directions may also require extra gates. These costs depend on both the circuit and the chosen placement. Logical qubits are variables in the algorithm; physical qubits are execution locations. Their correspondence can change during compilation. Problem 10.1. Why may a native gate count exceed a logical count? Worked solution A logical gate may require decomposition or routing. The two counts use different operation vocabularies."}, {"title": "Transpilation", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-2", "text": "10.2 Transpilation 10.2.1 Decomposition and synthesis Decomposition breaks a composite operation into smaller pieces. Synthesis constructs a sequence approximating a specified transformation within an error target. Transpilation can combine these with layout, routing, and simplification. Optimization levels select strategies; a larger level is not a proof of a smaller or better circuit for every instance. 10.2.2 Layout, routing, and optimization An initial layout assigns logical wires to physical positions. Routing may leave a final permutation. Comparing compiled matrices without accounting for that mapping can report a false error. The companion uses Operator.from_circuit so the circuit’s layout is interpreted, then checks equivalence with the original logical operator. Problem 10.2. What does an optimization level guarantee? Worked solution It selects a compilation strategy; it does not certify a globally minimal circuit or better physical performance on every backend."}, {"title": "Compiler Inspection", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-3", "text": "10.3 Compiler Inspection 10.3.1 Pass managers A pass manager organizes analysis and transformation passes. Fixing seed_transpiler makes randomized choices reproducible within the tested implementation. Inspect the resulting layout, operation counts, and depth rather than treating compilation as a black box. Run several seeds only if that search effort is included in the reported classical cost. 10.3.2 Logical equivalence and physical resource counts The example compiles a three-qubit circuit to rz, sx, x, and cx on a line. It prints logical and compiled counts and checks semantic equivalence. For larger circuits, full matrices may be impossible; use component proofs, structured tests, and observable checks with stated limits. Resource estimates should include added ancillas, routing, synthesis precision, and the target model. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_10.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 10.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, transpile\nfrom qiskit.transpiler import CouplingMap\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(3)\nqc.h(0)\nqc.cx(0, 2)\ncompiled = transpile(qc, basis_gates=[\"rz\", \"sx\", \"x\", \"cx\"],\n    coupling_map=CouplingMap.from_line(3), initial_layout=[0, 1, 2],\n    optimization_level=1, seed_transpiler=19)\nlogical = Operator.from_circuit(compiled)\nassert logical.equiv(Operator(qc))\nprint(\"Logical\", qc.count_ops(), \"compiled\", compiled.count_ops())\nprint(\"Layout:\", compiled.layout) Problem 10.3. Why record the layout with an experiment? Worked solution It tells which physical wires realize the logical ones and is needed to interpret observables and sometimes output mappings correctly. Compilation prepares a device-compatible workload. Hardware execution adds account access, budgets, and provenance. Sources and further study: IBM, transpiler API . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "Execution Setup", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-1", "text": "11.1 Execution Setup The same Bell circuit can be tested locally and submitted remotely. The two executions need different claims: a local simulation predicts an ideal model, while a hardware result reports a particular device at a particular time. 11.1.1 Backend selection and credentials Choose an accessible backend that supports the circuit’s width, instructions, and control requirements. Account setup and credentials belong outside source files. The companion uses a synthetic local backend by default. It does not access an account or submit a job unless the user explicitly supplies --submit and an exact --backend name. 11.1.2 Executable circuits and mapped observables Compile against the selected backend’s target before submission. For an Estimator workload, map each logical observable through the compiled circuit’s layout, for example with observable.apply_layout(compiled.layout). Otherwise the observable may refer to the wrong physical wires. The example uses a Sampler, whose measurement mapping is carried by the compiled circuit. Problem 11.1. Does importing the Runtime package submit a circuit? Worked solution No. A run call on a remote execution object is the submission action. Keep that action separated from module import and local testing."}, {"title": "Runtime Workloads", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-2", "text": "11.2 Runtime Workloads 11.2.1 Submitting and retrieving jobs A Runtime Sampler is constructed with the selected backend as its mode. Calling run submits the workload and returns a job handle. Save the job identifier immediately so a later process can recover the record. The submit branch below uses configured account access; it has not been run on hardware for this course edition. 11.2.2 Execution modes and budgets Job, session, and batch modes organize execution differently. Their availability and accounting depend on the service and account. Estimate circuits, shots, and expected device usage before submission; do not infer a fixed free quota. A short Python statement can launch substantial work. Keep the explicit submission boundary visible in reusable teaching code. Problem 11.2. Why map an Estimator observable after layout? Worked solution The logical qubits may have moved. Measuring Z on a different physical qubit can answer a different question even when the compiled state is correct."}, {"title": "Result Analysis", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-3", "text": "11.3 Result Analysis 11.3.1 Metadata and provenance Save the backend name, execution time, package versions, compiled circuit, layout, shots, options, and returned metadata. A count table without those facts cannot be reliably compared with another run. Device calibration drifts, so a later result may differ even with the same source circuit. Report what was actually executed rather than an intended logical circuit alone. 11.3.2 Simulation, hardware, and uncertainty The local path compiles against GenericBackendV2 and prints its resource counts. The remote path constructs an ISA circuit, submits 1024 shots, prints the job ID, and retrieves counts. Only the local path is included in automated checks. No hardware outcomes are invented; the example’s Bell-state ideal prediction remains 00 and 11 with equal probability, while a device may also produce errors. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_11.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 11.\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.transpiler import generate_preset_pass_manager\nfrom qiskit.providers.fake_provider import GenericBackendV2\nfrom qiskit_ibm_runtime import QiskitRuntimeService, SamplerV2\n\ndef build(backend):\n    circuit = QuantumCircuit(2)\n    circuit.h(0); circuit.cx(0, 1); circuit.measure_all()\n    return generate_preset_pass_manager(backend=backend, optimization_level=1,\n                                        seed_transpiler=17).run(circuit)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument(\"--submit\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"Explicitly submit a paid/allocated hardware job\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--backend\", help=\"Exact accessible hardware backend name\")\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    if not args.submit:\n        compiled = build(GenericBackendV2(2, seed=3))\n        print(\"Local compilation only; no account accessed and no job submitted.\")\n        print(compiled.count_ops())\n    else:\n        if not args.backend:\n            parser.error(\"--backend is required with --submit\")\n        service = QiskitRuntimeService()  # Uses a separately configured account.\n        backend = service.backend(args.backend)\n        compiled = build(backend)\n        job = SamplerV2(mode=backend).run([compiled], shots=1024)\n        print(\"Submitted job:\", job.job_id())\n        print(job.result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()) Problem 11.3. What is missing from a claim that hardware “gave 95% success”? Worked solution The task, success definition, shots and uncertainty, device and time, circuit and layout, execution options, and comparison baseline are all needed. Hardware can sometimes act on measurement results before a circuit finishes. We next program that feedback explicitly. Sources and further study: IBM, Runtime SamplerV2 ."}, {"title": "Mid-Circuit Operations", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-1", "text": "12.1 Mid-Circuit Operations Teleportation is a demanding program test because every measurement branch must recover the same input state. Averaging only the easiest branch would hide a broken correction rule. 12.1.1 Measurement and reset A mid-circuit measurement writes a classical bit while other quantum wires remain available. Reset prepares a wire in zero but can destroy its previous correlations. These are physical operations, not Python variable assignments. The reference StatevectorSampler is intended for suitable final-measurement workloads; the companion uses Aer for measurement-conditioned circuit execution. 12.1.2 Classical conditions and feedback The with circuit.if_test((bit,1)) context appends an operation conditioned on a classical record. A host-language if cannot inspect the result of an unexecuted circuit. The condition refers to a classical bit or register, so use the exact object and encoding intended by the measurement map. Problem 12.1. Why apply Z controlled by c0 rather than c1? Worked solution The H measurement on the original input determines the phase correction. The Bell-half measurement determines the bit-flip correction. Swapping these records fails on general input states."}, {"title": "Conditional Programs", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-2", "text": "12.2 Conditional Programs 12.2.1 Teleportation The example places the input on q0, Alice’s Bell half on q1, and Bob’s on q2. After CX(0,1) and H(0), Alice measures q0 into c0 and q1 into c1. Bob applies X when c1 is one and then Z when c0 is one. These corrections invert the branch operator X^c1 Z^c0 up to an irrelevant branch phase. 12.2.2 Iterative procedures For a known test input prepared by Ry(0.7) then Rz(0.4), apply its inverse on Bob after correction. Measuring Bob should then give zero in every branch. This verifies a state with nontrivial amplitudes and relative phase. Repeat with other preparations or an entangled reference for stronger channel validation; one test state is not complete process tomography. Problem 12.2. Does Bob receive the state before Alice communicates her outcomes? Worked solution Without the records his state is averaged over the four Pauli branches. The corrections require those classical bits, so the protocol does not signal instantly. Teleportation with classical feedforward. Alice’s top measurement gives m and the middle gives n; Bob applies X^n then Z^m using the communicated records. No quantum control from the measured wires is implied."}, {"title": "Execution Constraints", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-3", "text": "12.3 Execution Constraints 12.3.1 Simulator and backend capabilities A simulator accepting if_test does not prove a selected device supports the same dynamic circuit. Check backend capabilities, supported conditions, reset behavior, and timing constraints before submission. The companion runs locally and does not imply hardware availability. A controller’s limited classical expressions may require rewriting a high-level condition. 12.3.2 Latency, branches, and correctness Feedback can save qubits or coherent depth, but adds measurement and classical latency. In the counts below, bit strings are c2 c1 c0; c2 is the final verification result and must be zero. The lower two bits vary because Alice’s Bell measurement is random. We assert correctness of every observed branch, not only the aggregate fraction of convenient outcomes. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_12.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 12.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit_aer import AerSimulator\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, transpile\n\n# q0=input, q1=Alice's half, q2=Bob's half; c0,c1 are correction records.\nqc = QuantumCircuit(3, 3)\nqc.ry(0.7, 0); qc.rz(0.4, 0)\nqc.h(1); qc.cx(1, 2)\nqc.cx(0, 1); qc.h(0)\nqc.measure(0, 0); qc.measure(1, 1)\nwith qc.if_test((qc.clbits[1], 1)):\n    qc.x(2)\nwith qc.if_test((qc.clbits[0], 1)):\n    qc.z(2)\n# Inverse of the known test preparation verifies the recovered state.\nqc.rz(-0.4, 2); qc.ry(-0.7, 2)\nqc.measure(2, 2)\nsim = AerSimulator(seed_simulator=23, max_parallel_threads=1)\ncounts = sim.run(transpile(qc, sim), shots=1024).result().get_counts()\nassert all(key[0] == \"0\" for key in counts)\nprint(\"c2 c1 c0:\", counts) Problem 12.3. Why test a nontrivial phase as well as real amplitudes? Worked solution A missing Z correction may be invisible on a basis state. A phase-sensitive input exposes that error. Correct dynamic logic can still fail under noise. The next chapter separates noise models from sampling and approximation. Sources and further study: IBM, classical feedforward ."}, {"title": "Noise Models", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-1", "text": "13.1 Noise Models An X gate should produce one from zero, yet a noisy device sometimes reports zero. Before attempting a correction, distinguish an erroneous gate from an erroneous readout and from finite sampling fluctuations. 13.1.1 Gate and readout errors A gate error changes the quantum state before measurement; a readout error changes the reported classical label. The example attaches a depolarizing channel to X: E(ρ)=0.9ρ+0.1I/2. On the intended one state, the measured probability of one is 0.95. The parameter 0.1 is not itself the probability of a wrong reported bit under this convention. 13.1.2 Constructing noisy simulations NoiseModel assigns errors to instruction names and qubits. If transpilation removes or renames an instruction, a model attached to its old name may no longer act where intended. Inspect the compiled circuit. The companion uses optimization_level=0 and an explicit X experiment so the attachment remains clear. A chosen model is a hypothesis about errors, not a calibrated device by itself. Problem 13.1. For depolarization strength 0.2 after X, predict P(1). Worked solution The state is 0.8|1〉〈1|+0.2I/2, so P(1)=0.9."}, {"title": "Reliability Techniques", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-2", "text": "13.2 Reliability Techniques 13.2.1 Error suppression Suppression attempts to reduce errors during execution: better layouts, shorter circuits, or properly scheduled decoupling can help under appropriate noise assumptions. Adding gates can also make errors worse. Compare a controlled baseline and count the added duration and operations rather than assuming that a named technique always helps. 13.2.2 Error mitigation and its assumptions Mitigation estimates an ideal quantity from noisy data, often by calibration, inversion, or extrapolation. For symmetric readout flip probability e, the observed Z mean is (1−2e) times the true mean; dividing by that factor corrects the mean if the model is right. Its variance grows by 1/(1−2e)^2, diverging as e approaches one half. Calibration uncertainty and drift must also be included. Problem 13.2. At readout flip rate e=0.1, how does inverse correction scale variance? Worked solution It multiplies variance by 1/0.8²=1.5625, even if e is known exactly."}, {"title": "Interpreting Discrepancies", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-3", "text": "13.3 Interpreting Discrepancies 13.3.1 Noise, approximation, and sampling A discrepancy can come from a wrong program, an approximate circuit, random shots, or a misspecified noise model. Check them in that order with increasingly realistic experiments. More shots reduce random fluctuation but do not fix a biased model. Comparing an exact simulator with noisy hardware without those distinctions is not a diagnosis. 13.3.2 Bias, uncertainty, and reporting limits The companion runs 8192 simulated shots and checks the frequency against 0.95 with a conservative tolerance. It does not claim that depolarization describes every device or that the same tolerance is a confidence interval for all experiments. Report the model, parameter convention, compiled gate attachment, seed, and shot count with the output. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_13.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 13.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit_aer import AerSimulator\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, transpile\nfrom qiskit_aer.noise import NoiseModel, depolarizing_error\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(1)\nqc.x(0); qc.measure_all()\nnoise = NoiseModel()\nnoise.add_all_qubit_quantum_error(depolarizing_error(0.1, 1), [\"x\"])\nsim = AerSimulator(noise_model=noise, seed_simulator=31, max_parallel_threads=1)\ncounts = sim.run(transpile(qc, sim, optimization_level=0), shots=8192).result().get_counts()\np1 = counts.get(\"1\", 0) / 8192\n# E(rho) = .9 rho + .1 I/2, so expected P(1) = .95.\nassert abs(p1 - 0.95) < 0.03\nprint(\"Simulated P(1):\", p1, \"analytic prediction: 0.95\") Problem 13.3. Can error mitigation certify a fault-tolerant computation? Worked solution No. Mitigation depends on a noise model and statistical processing. It does not supply an error-correcting logical computation or a general guarantee against arbitrary errors. The next program uses repeated estimates to choose parameters, making the statistical budget part of the optimization loop. Sources and further study: IBM, noise model construction ."}, {"title": "Parameterized Workflows", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-1", "text": "14.1 Parameterized Workflows A variational program repeatedly prepares a parameterized state and asks for its energy. Start with an objective whose entire curve is known, so the optimizer cannot conceal an implementation mistake. 14.1.1 Parameter binding and objectives Prepare cos(θ/2)|00〉+sin(θ/2)|11〉 using Ry on qubit zero followed by CNOT. For H=−ZZ−XX, the energy is −1−sin θ. The minimum −2 occurs at θ=π/2. This analytic curve is the oracle for testing the program; the numerical search should reproduce it before any larger problem is attempted. 14.1.2 Repeated estimation Keep the circuit symbolic and bind parameters through the primitive’s input array. Each evaluation has a cost, even if the host loop is short. The companion evaluates a grid in one local Estimator workload. Exact local expectations remove shot noise from this first debugging stage; a hardware experiment would need a measurement allocation and confidence analysis. Problem 14.1. Why is the energy at θ=0 equal to −1? Worked solution The state is 00, so ZZ has expectation one and XX has expectation zero. A one-parameter entangled trial state: cos(θ/2)|00⟩+sin(θ/2)|11⟩. Measure ZZ directly or rotate both wires with H before measuring XX."}, {"title": "Optimization Loops", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-2", "text": "14.2 Optimization Loops 14.2.1 Gradients and classical updates For a single Pauli-generated rotation, the derivative is half the difference of energies at θ±π/2. A gradient step subtracts a learning-rate multiple of that derivative. A grid search is simpler for the one-parameter example and gives a reproducible baseline. Large parameter spaces require more careful optimization, but complicated optimizers do not fix a wrong circuit or objective. 14.2.2 Stopping criteria and reproducibility Stop according to an energy-improvement and uncertainty criterion, plus an evaluation budget. Fix initial points and random seeds when comparing methods. A noisy objective can have biased gradients and flat regions. Record failed runs and initialization sensitivity; choosing only the best seed after many attempts understates classical and quantum costs. Problem 14.2. Can a measured energy below −2 be accepted as a better solution? Worked solution Not for this exact Hamiltonian. It indicates statistical fluctuation, bias, or a programming error; the true minimum is −2."}, {"title": "Applications", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-3", "text": "14.3 Applications 14.3.1 Variational eigensolvers A variational eigensolver is a trial family, observable estimator, and classical parameter search. The variational bound says each exact trial energy is at least the ground energy; it does not guarantee that the family contains the ground state or that optimization finds its best member. Noisy estimates can violate that bound numerically without violating quantum mechanics. 14.3.2 QAOA and performance comparisons QAOA changes the trial family to alternating cost and mixer evolutions. Compare its output distribution with a classical heuristic on the same instances and include training evaluations. The companion’s solved two-qubit energy demonstrates a correct loop interface, not quantum advantage. It verifies the whole sampled parameter grid against the analytic curve and reports the minimum. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_14.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 14.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorEstimator\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import SparsePauliOp\n\ntheta = Parameter(\"theta\")\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.ry(theta, 0); qc.cx(0, 1)\nH = SparsePauliOp.from_list([(\"ZZ\", -1), (\"XX\", -1)])\nangles = np.linspace(0, np.pi, 101)\nenergies = StatevectorEstimator().run([(qc, H, angles[:, None])]).result()[0].data.evs\nassert np.allclose(energies, -1 - np.sin(angles))\nbest = int(np.argmin(energies))\nassert np.isclose(energies[best], -2)\nprint(\"Grid minimum:\", angles[best], energies[best]) Problem 14.3. Why keep a grid baseline? Worked solution It provides a transparent reference on the small problem and exposes errors in sophisticated optimization machinery. Its cost grows rapidly with parameter dimension, so it is not a general scalable strategy. The next chapter implements operator constructions whose approximation and normalization must be tested as carefully as their code."}, {"title": "Hamiltonian Simulation", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-1", "text": "15.1 Hamiltonian Simulation Advanced circuit boxes often hide two separate issues: how accurately they implement a target transformation, and how often a selected auxiliary outcome occurs. We will expose both with small matrices. 15.1.1 Product-formula circuits For H=X+Z and time one, alternate Rz(2/r) then Rx(2/r), repeated r times. Since Rz(θ)=exp(−iθZ/2), this realizes (exp(−iX/r)exp(−iZ/r))^r. Compare its operator with exp(−i(X+Z)), computed classically only for this small verification instance. The gate order is chosen deliberately to match the formula. 15.1.2 Time and approximation error The first-order norm bound is at most 1/r for this example. The companion checks r=10,20,40 against that bound. It reports approximation error separately from sampling and hardware noise. Increasing r reduces the formula error but adds gates, so on hardware the best r can be finite rather than as large as possible. Problem 15.1. Why is the rotation angle 2/r rather than 1/r? Worked solution Qiskit’s Pauli rotation convention has a factor one half in the exponential. Using 1/r would simulate half the intended term strength."}, {"title": "Operator Embeddings", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-2", "text": "15.2 Operator Embeddings 15.2.1 Ancillas and normalization A block encoding of A/α places that transformation in the zero-ancilla block of a unitary. The scale α and ancilla count are part of the interface. Successful projection produces A|ψ〉/α before normalization. A matrix entry check verifies the encoding, while a state-specific probability check tells how expensive selection will be. 15.2.2 Linear combinations of unitaries For A=(I+X)/2, use an auxiliary H, controlled-X, then H. In the companion, auxiliary q1 is the high bit, so the selected block is the top-left two-by-two block. Reversing the ancilla and system indices changes which matrix slice is correct. Randomly choosing I or X would not implement this coherent sum. Problem 15.2. What is the success probability of the LCU example on system zero? Worked solution The selected vector is (|0〉+|1〉)/2, with squared norm one half. The normalized conditional state is plus. An encoding of (I+X)/2. Select ancilla outcome zero to obtain the desired sum on the system; success depends on the input state."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-3", "text": "15.3 Polynomial Transformations 15.3.1 Phase sequences Signal processing alternates an encoded signal with phase rotations. Fix the sign convention, ordering, selected auxiliary block, and admissible polynomial before importing a phase list. For the scalar signal W(x)=xI+i√(1−x²)X, two calls give response 2x²−1. Repeating a block encoding does not generally square its selected block, because unselected amplitudes can return. 15.3.2 Verification and compilation costs Validate a candidate polynomial at sample points and at its promised interval boundaries, then test the matrix action on small encoded examples. Sampling is not a proof of a uniform approximation bound, which must come from the construction. Charge encoding calls, normalization, success amplification, phase synthesis, and compilation. The companion verifies product formulas and the basic LCU block used by those more advanced constructions. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_15.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 15.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\n\nX = np.array([[0, 1], [1, 0]])\nZ = np.diag([1, -1])\nerrors = []\nfor steps in (10, 20, 40):\n    qc = QuantumCircuit(1)\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        qc.rz(2/steps, 0)\n        qc.rx(2/steps, 0)\n    error = np.linalg.norm(Operator(qc).data - expm(-1j*(X+Z)), 2)\n    assert error <= 1/steps + 1e-12\n    errors.append(error)\n# An LCU encoding of (I+X)/2: ancilla q1, system q0.\nlcu = QuantumCircuit(2)\nlcu.h(1); lcu.cx(1, 0); lcu.h(1)\nassert np.allclose(Operator(lcu).data[:2, :2], (np.eye(2)+X)/2)\nprint(\"Product-formula errors:\", errors)\n\n# Verify the low-degree signal response independently of a synthesis package.\nfor x in np.linspace(-1, 1, 21):\n    W = x*np.eye(2) + 1j*np.sqrt(max(0, 1-x*x))*X\n    assert np.allclose(W.conj().T @ W, np.eye(2))\n    assert np.isclose((W @ W)[0, 0], 2*x*x-1) Problem 15.3. Why cannot a degree count alone specify total cost? Worked solution Each signal call may contain a large encoding circuit, and success amplification or gate synthesis adds work. The access implementation matters. The final chapter packages these habits into a reproducible project rather than an isolated successful run."}, {"title": "Project Structure", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-1", "text": "16.1 Project Structure A project is reproducible when another reader can reconstruct its task, circuit, assumptions, and evidence. A plot without the program and execution record is not enough. 16.1.1 Modules, notebooks, and tests Keep circuit factories, experiment drivers, and tests separate. A notebook can explain an experiment, while a script runs it from a fresh process. Tests should include a small independently solvable case, asymmetric ordering checks, and phase-sensitive inputs. Never make importing a module submit a hardware job. 16.1.2 Dependencies and version control Pin tested dependencies and record the Python version. Keep credentials out of version control. Record source changes so that a result points to the code that produced it. A requirements file captures direct dependencies; a full environment lock and platform note improve exact reproduction. Updating a package requires rerunning relevant examples rather than assuming unchanged behavior. Problem 16.1. Why does the target bit vary in the capstone output? Worked solution It remains in minus, which gives a fair computational-basis result. The useful secret is in the other bits, so the decoder excludes the target bit."}, {"title": "Experiment Records", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-2", "text": "16.2 Experiment Records 16.2.1 Saved circuits and configurations Save a circuit artifact as well as its drawing. QPY preserves Qiskit circuit structure for supported version compatibility, and the companion checks an in-memory save/load round trip. Keep the logical circuit, compiled circuit, layout, parameter values, and seeds when compilation is involved. A diagram alone may omit global phases or parameter metadata. 16.2.2 Results, metadata, and execution costs An experiment record should identify the problem, input size, access assumptions, success criterion, shots, qubits, logical and compiled counts, execution type, package versions, and measured outputs. Exact local simulation, noisy simulation, and hardware must be labeled distinctly. Confidence intervals and error budgets belong beside conclusions, not hidden in raw logs. Problem 16.2. What changes if the secret is all zeros? Worked solution The oracle has no CNOTs and the input Hadamards cancel, so the output secret is all zeros. This is an important edge case."}, {"title": "Capstone Project", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-3", "text": "16.3 Capstone Project 16.3.1 Specification and implementation The capstone implements Bernstein–Vazirani for an arbitrary supplied binary secret. It prepares a minus target, uses one CNOT for each one bit in the oracle description, applies Hadamards, and reads the input register. The known secret lets us verify construction; it is not being discovered by a program that lacks access to its own source. The algorithmic query model treats the constructed oracle as the supplied interface. 16.3.2 Evaluation, limitations, and documentation The companion uses secret 1011, checks circuit serialization, samples, and verifies every input-register result. The target is measured too and appears at the left of Qiskit’s full string; bits[1:] extracts the input register. Extend the project across lengths, test zero and all-one secrets, report oracle gate growth, and compare with the classical query baseline under the same oracle contract. The result is a reproducible implementation study, not a hardware speedup claim. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_16.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 16.\"\"\"\nimport io, json, sys\nfrom importlib.metadata import version\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, qpy\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nsecret = \"1011\"  # Written high bit first.\nn = len(secret)\nqc = QuantumCircuit(n+1)\nqc.x(n); qc.h(range(n+1))\nfor qubit, bit in enumerate(reversed(secret)):\n    if bit == \"1\":\n        qc.cx(qubit, n)\nqc.h(range(n))\n# Save/load the coherent circuit before appending measurements.\nbuffer = io.BytesIO()\nqpy.dump(qc, buffer); buffer.seek(0)\nrestored = qpy.load(buffer)[0]\nassert Operator(qc).equiv(Operator(restored))\nqc.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=41).run([qc], shots=1024).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nassert all(bits[1:] == secret for bits in counts)\nrecord = {\"problem\": \"Bernstein-Vazirani\", \"secret\": secret,\n    \"python\": sys.version.split()[0], \"qiskit\": version(\"qiskit\"),\n    \"seed\": 41, \"shots\": 1024, \"qubits\": qc.num_qubits,\n    \"gate_counts\": dict(qc.count_ops()), \"counts\": counts,\n    \"execution\": \"local ideal StatevectorSampler\", \"oracle_queries_per_shot\": 1}\nprint(json.dumps(record, indent=2)) Problem 16.3. What evidence would be needed for a runtime advantage claim? Worked solution A scalable task and access model, matched classical baseline, complete preparation and oracle costs, execution and readout costs, comparable success and precision, and measurements or justified resource analysis over growing sizes. This completes the programming sequence. The companion examples form a locally tested starting point for projects, with hardware submission kept explicit and separately documented. Sources and further study: IBM, QPY serialization . Phase-oracle readout. U_f is the reversible bit oracle on the input register and the minus target, not an elementary gate. The final input measurement reads the encoded pattern."}];
